{
  "items": [
    {
      "id": "emp_cap",
      "title": "Community-acquired pneumonia (CAP)",
      "category": "Respiratory",
      "subtitle": "Adults | outpatient, ward and ICU",
      "summary": "Choose the regimen by severity and by comorbidity. Cover pneumococcus and atypicals, add MRSA or Pseudomonas cover only when there is a specific risk, and treat for at least 5 days.",
      "sections": [
        {
          "title": "Outpatient, no comorbidities",
          "text": "• Amoxicillin 1 g orally three times a day, or doxycycline 100 mg orally every 12 hours.\n• A macrolide (azithromycin 500 mg on day 1, then 250 mg daily; or clarithromycin 500 mg every 12 hours) is an option only where local pneumococcal macrolide resistance is below 25%."
        },
        {
          "title": "Outpatient, with comorbidities",
          "text": "• Comorbidities: chronic heart, lung, liver or renal disease, diabetes, alcohol use disorder, malignancy, asplenia.\n• Combination: amoxicillin-clavulanate (500/125 mg three times a day, or 875/125 mg twice a day) or a cephalosporin (cefpodoxime 200 mg twice a day, or cefuroxime 500 mg twice a day), plus a macrolide or doxycycline.\n• Alternative: monotherapy with a respiratory fluoroquinolone (levofloxacin 750 mg once a day or moxifloxacin 400 mg once a day), reserved for when beta-lactam combinations cannot be used."
        },
        {
          "title": "Inpatient, non-severe",
          "text": "• Beta-lactam plus macrolide: ampicillin-sulbactam 1.5 to 3 g IV every 6 hours, ceftriaxone 1 to 2 g IV once a day, cefotaxime 1 to 2 g IV every 8 hours, or ceftaroline 600 mg IV every 12 hours; plus azithromycin 500 mg once a day or clarithromycin 500 mg every 12 hours.\n• Alternative: respiratory fluoroquinolone monotherapy (levofloxacin 750 mg once a day or moxifloxacin 400 mg once a day).\n• If both a macrolide and a fluoroquinolone are unsuitable: beta-lactam plus doxycycline 100 mg every 12 hours."
        },
        {
          "title": "Severe CAP (ICU)",
          "text": "• Beta-lactam plus macrolide, or beta-lactam plus a respiratory fluoroquinolone; monotherapy is not recommended.\n• Beta-lactam choices: ampicillin-sulbactam, cefotaxime, ceftriaxone or ceftaroline, at the doses above.\n• Start immediately: delay in antibiotics worsens outcome in severe disease."
        },
        {
          "title": "Add-on cover and testing",
          "text": "• Prior MRSA or Pseudomonas isolation from the respiratory tract: send cultures and add cover. MRSA: vancomycin or linezolid. Pseudomonas: piperacillin-tazobactam, cefepime, ceftazidime, meropenem, imipenem or aztreonam.\n• Recent hospitalisation with IV antibiotics in the last 90 days: in severe CAP add MRSA and Pseudomonas cover and send cultures; in non-severe CAP send cultures and add cover only if they turn positive.\n• Send a nasal MRSA PCR in suspected MRSA: a negative result lets you stop MRSA cover.\n• Test for influenza in season and give oseltamivir if positive or strongly suspected, even without waiting for results in sick patients.\n• Legionella urine antigen and PCR in severe disease or epidemiologic clues; ensure atypical cover.\n• Aspiration alone does not need added anaerobic cover, except with lung abscess or empyema."
        },
        {
          "title": "Duration and review",
          "text": "• At least 5 days of antibiotics, and stop once the patient is clinically stable (afebrile for 48 to 72 hours with no more than one sign of instability).\n• Switch to oral therapy when stable and able to absorb; do not extend because of residual cough.\n• No routine follow-up chest X-ray in adults who recover; consider it in smokers over 50 to exclude malignancy.\n• Corticosteroids: not routinely for non-severe CAP; evidence in severe CAP is evolving (for example hydrocortisone in recent trials), so follow local guidance."
        },
        {
          "title": "Allergy and special situations",
          "text": "• Penicillin allergy with low-risk history: a cephalosporin plus a macrolide is usually acceptable; otherwise a respiratory fluoroquinolone.\n• Pregnancy: avoid fluoroquinolones and doxycycline; use a beta-lactam plus azithromycin.\n• Check local resistance and the most recent ATS/IDSA update before applying these regimens."
        }
      ],
      "source": "ATS/IDSA 2019 CAP guideline. Doses are standard adult doses; verify against current guidance and local resistance data."
    },
    {
      "id": "emp_hap_vap",
      "title": "Hospital-acquired and ventilator-associated pneumonia",
      "category": "Respiratory",
      "subtitle": "Adults | HAP onset after 48 hours in hospital; VAP after 48 hours of ventilation",
      "summary": "Always cover Pseudomonas and other Gram-negatives, and add MRSA cover when risk is present. Use local antibiograms, send cultures, de-escalate early and treat for 7 days.",
      "sections": [
        {
          "title": "HAP, low risk",
          "text": "• No MRSA risk factors and not at high risk of death: one antipseudomonal agent is enough.\n• Choices: piperacillin-tazobactam 4.5 g IV every 6 hours, cefepime 2 g IV every 8 hours, levofloxacin 750 mg IV once a day, imipenem 500 mg IV every 6 hours, or meropenem 1 g IV every 8 hours."
        },
        {
          "title": "HAP with MRSA risk or high mortality risk; and VAP",
          "text": "• Antipseudomonal beta-lactam (as above, or ceftazidime 2 g every 8 hours or aztreonam 2 g every 8 hours) plus MRSA cover: vancomycin (loading dose, then level-guided dosing, see the TDM tab) or linezolid 600 mg every 12 hours.\n• VAP: guidelines recommend MRSA and double Gram-negative cover when MDR risk factors are present, or when the unit's resistance to the single agent is above 10%.\n• Second antipseudomonal agent (different class): ciprofloxacin 400 mg every 8 hours, levofloxacin 750 mg once a day, or an aminoglycoside such as amikacin or gentamicin; avoid colistin or polymyxin B unless needed (see the Polymyxins monograph).\n• Avoid two beta-lactams together."
        },
        {
          "title": "Risk factors for resistant organisms",
          "text": "• IV antibiotics in the previous 90 days.\n• Septic shock, or ARDS preceding the pneumonia.\n• 5 or more days of hospitalisation before onset, or acute renal replacement therapy before onset.\n• Known colonisation or prior infection with a resistant organism.\n• Treatment in a unit where more than 10 to 20% of isolates are resistant to the agent chosen."
        },
        {
          "title": "Testing and de-escalation",
          "text": "• Send lower respiratory samples for culture before antibiotics if this causes no delay.\n• A negative MRSA nasal PCR has a high negative predictive value, so MRSA cover can be stopped.\n• De-escalate to a narrow agent as soon as culture results and susceptibility are available.\n• Review daily; stop agents that are not needed."
        },
        {
          "title": "Duration",
          "text": "• 7 days of effective therapy for most patients, rather than 14 days.\n• Procalcitonin or clinical criteria can guide stopping.\n• Longer courses may be needed for empyema, lung abscess, or slow response, and for difficult-to-treat Gram-negatives.\n• Review any patient not improving by 48 to 72 hours: consider resistant organisms, empyema, other sources and non-infectious causes."
        },
        {
          "title": "Resistant organisms",
          "text": "• Carbapenem-resistant Enterobacterales, Pseudomonas, Acinetobacter and Stenotrophomonas: see the IDSA AMR tab for preferred regimens.\n• Newer agents (ceftazidime-avibactam, ceftolozane-tazobactam, cefiderocol, sulbactam-durlobactam) are described in the Drugs tab.\n• Daptomycin is inactive in the lung and must not be used for pneumonia.\n• Check the latest IDSA/ATS guidance for updates to HAP/VAP management."
        }
      ],
      "source": "IDSA/ATS 2016 HAP and VAP guideline. Doses are standard adult doses; verify against current guidance and local antibiograms."
    },
    {
      "id": "emp_cystitis",
      "title": "Uncomplicated cystitis",
      "category": "Urinary",
      "subtitle": "Non-pregnant adult women | outpatient",
      "summary": "Short oral courses of nitrofurantoin, fosfomycin, pivmecillinam or TMP-SMX. Avoid fluoroquinolones and broad beta-lactams unless there is no alternative. Do not treat asymptomatic bacteriuria.",
      "sections": [
        {
          "title": "First-line options",
          "text": "• Nitrofurantoin (macrocrystals/monohydrate) 100 mg orally every 12 hours for 5 days.\n• Fosfomycin trometamol 3 g orally as a single dose.\n• Pivmecillinam 200 mg orally three times a day for 3 days (UK practice uses a 400 mg first dose).\n• Trimethoprim-sulfamethoxazole one double-strength tablet every 12 hours for 3 days, only if local E. coli resistance is below about 20% and the patient has not used it in the past 3 to 6 months."
        },
        {
          "title": "Newer options",
          "text": "• Gepotidacin 1500 mg orally twice a day for 5 days.\n• Sulopenem etzadroxil/probenecid 500 mg/500 mg orally twice a day for 5 days.\n• Both are recent approvals: check the product label and local availability before use."
        },
        {
          "title": "Second-line and avoid",
          "text": "• Beta-lactams are less effective: cephalexin 500 mg every 12 hours for 3 to 7 days, or amoxicillin-clavulanate 500/125 mg every 12 hours for 3 to 7 days.\n• Fluoroquinolones (ciprofloxacin 250 mg every 12 hours or levofloxacin 250 mg once a day for 3 days) should be reserved for when other agents cannot be used, because of boxed-warning adverse effects and resistance.\n• Do not use nitrofurantoin or fosfomycin for pyelonephritis, prostatitis or bacteraemia."
        },
        {
          "title": "When to send a urine culture",
          "text": "• Symptoms persist or recur within 2 to 4 weeks.\n• Atypical symptoms, pregnancy, recent antibiotic use or recent travel to areas with high resistance.\n• Men, children, older adults with catheters, or anyone with a complicated urinary tract.\n• Recurrent cystitis: culture each episode, and consider prevention strategies with specialist advice."
        },
        {
          "title": "Pregnancy",
          "text": "• Treat both symptomatic and asymptomatic bacteriuria in pregnancy.\n• Usually acceptable: cephalexin, amoxicillin-clavulanate and fosfomycin, chosen by culture.\n• Avoid nitrofurantoin near term (38 to 42 weeks) and trimethoprim-sulfamethoxazole in the first trimester and near term; avoid fluoroquinolones and tetracyclines."
        },
        {
          "title": "Do not treat",
          "text": "• Asymptomatic bacteriuria in non-pregnant adults, including older adults and catheterised patients.\n• Exceptions: pregnancy, and before urological procedures that breach the mucosa.\n• A positive dipstick or odorous urine without symptoms is not an indication for antibiotics."
        }
      ],
      "source": "IDSA cystitis guidance, EAU/AUA guidance and product labelling. Verify against current guidance and local resistance."
    },
    {
      "id": "emp_pyelonephritis",
      "title": "Pyelonephritis and complicated UTI",
      "category": "Urinary",
      "subtitle": "Adults | outpatient and inpatient, including catheter-associated",
      "summary": "Send urine and blood cultures, start an agent with reliable tissue levels, and tailor to susceptibility. Look for obstruction or abscess in patients who are septic or slow to improve.",
      "sections": [
        {
          "title": "Outpatient, mild to moderate",
          "text": "• Oral fluoroquinolone if local resistance is low (below about 10%): ciprofloxacin 500 mg every 12 hours for 7 days, or levofloxacin 750 mg once a day for 5 days.\n• Trimethoprim-sulfamethoxazole one double-strength tablet every 12 hours, if the isolate is susceptible, often for 7 to 14 days.\n• Oral beta-lactams are less effective: if used, give a first dose of IV ceftriaxone 1 g and continue for 10 to 14 days.\n• Do not use nitrofurantoin or fosfomycin."
        },
        {
          "title": "Inpatient or sepsis",
          "text": "• Ceftriaxone 1 to 2 g IV once a day, or cefepime 1 to 2 g IV every 12 hours (2 g every 8 hours in severe sepsis), or piperacillin-tazobactam 3.375 to 4.5 g IV every 6 hours.\n• Aminoglycoside once daily (gentamicin or tobramycin 5 to 7 mg/kg) as an alternative or in severe sepsis, short courses only.\n• Fluoroquinolone IV (ciprofloxacin 400 mg every 12 hours or levofloxacin 750 mg once a day) if local resistance is low.\n• Prior ESBL infection, recent travel or other risk: start a carbapenem (ertapenem 1 g once a day or meropenem 1 g every 8 hours)."
        },
        {
          "title": "ESBL and resistant organisms",
          "text": "• ESBL-producing Enterobacterales (cUTI): trimethoprim-sulfamethoxazole, ciprofloxacin or levofloxacin if susceptible; carbapenem for severe infection or when those agents cannot be used; aminoglycosides are an alternative.\n• Carbapenem-resistant Enterobacterales and Pseudomonas: see the IDSA AMR tab for dosing.\n• Switch to oral therapy as soon as the patient is stable and the isolate is susceptible."
        },
        {
          "title": "Source control and imaging",
          "text": "• Obstructed infected kidney (stone, tumour) is a urological emergency: decompress with a stent or nephrostomy.\n• Image (ultrasound or CT) if septic, in renal failure, in diabetes with severe illness, with a history of stones, or if not improving by 48 to 72 hours.\n• Look for abscess, emphysematous pyelonephritis and prostatitis in men.\n• Remove or replace long-term catheters, ideally before starting therapy."
        },
        {
          "title": "Catheter-associated UTI",
          "text": "• Treat only if symptomatic; replace the catheter after 2 weeks or more of use.\n• Take the culture from the new catheter, or from a freshly placed specimen.\n• Duration: 7 days for prompt response, and 10 to 14 days for delayed response.\n• Women 65 or younger without upper-tract symptoms after catheter removal: 3 days may be enough."
        },
        {
          "title": "Duration",
          "text": "• About 7 days for patients who improve promptly, with a range of 5 to 14 days depending on the agent and response.\n• Men with possible prostatitis: 2 to 4 weeks with an agent that reaches the prostate (fluoroquinolone or trimethoprim-sulfamethoxazole).\n• Bacteraemia from a urinary source usually needs 7 days if there is a prompt response and source control."
        },
        {
          "title": "Pregnancy",
          "text": "• Admit and start IV therapy, usually ceftriaxone 1 to 2 g once a day; avoid fluoroquinolones and doxycycline.\n• Consider suppressive therapy for the rest of pregnancy after an episode, according to local guidance.\n• Check urine culture after treatment."
        }
      ],
      "source": "IDSA pyelonephritis and complicated UTI guidance, IDSA AMR guidance and product labelling. Verify against current guidance and local resistance."
    }
  ]
}